{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q104381574","formula":"C₁₂H₁₈BrNO₂","hill":"C12H18BrNO2","elements":["H","C","N","O","Br"],"composition":{"C":12.0,"H":18.0,"N":1.0,"O":2.0,"Br":1.0},"names":{"en":"4C-B"},"descriptions":{"en":"4C-B, also known as 4C-DOB or DOB-B, as well as 4-bromo-2,5-dimethoxy-α-ethylphenethylamine, is a lesser-known psychedelic drug of the phenethylamine, phenylisobutylamine, and 4C families related to 2C-B (the 2C analogue) and DOB (the DOx analogue). It is briefly mentioned in Alexander Shulgin's 1991 book PiHKAL (Phenethylamines I Have Known And Loved), but was never tested by Shulgin. Subsequently, the drug was tested by Daniel Trachsel and colleagues, and was found to be active in a dose range of 50 to 80 mg orally with a duration of around 8 hours."},"fame":1,"pubchemCid":12140147,"mass":288.18,"charge":0,"smiles":"CCC(CC1=CC(=C(C=C1OC)Br)OC)N","inchikey":"QQPRORAZQWLMTQ-UHFFFAOYSA-N","iupac":"1-(4-bromo-2,5-dimethoxyphenyl)butan-2-amine","classes":[{"qid":"Q113145171","names":{"en":"type of chemical entity","ru":"тип химического объекта","uk":"тип хімічної сутності"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"molecule","source":"pubchem","ref":"12140147","atoms":34,"license":"PubChem (NIH), public data"},"structure":{"kind":"molecule","atoms":[{"x":3.998,"y":1.33,"z":-0.889,"el":"Br"},{"x":-0.943,"y":2.058,"z":0.618,"el":"O"},{"x":3.092,"y":-1.592,"z":-0.28,"el":"O"},{"x":-3.467,"y":-2.003,"z":0.473,"el":"N"},{"x":-2.469,"y":-1.058,"z":-0.041,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.487,"y":-0.706,"z":1.095,"el":"C"},{"x":-3.173,"y":0.189,"z":-0.608,"el":"C"},{"x":-0.148,"y":-0.202,"z":0.61,"el":"C"},{"x":0.051,"y":1.153,"z":0.398,"el":"C"},{"x":-4.176,"y":-0.133,"z":-1.71,"el":"C"},{"x":0.865,"y":-1.122,"z":0.384,"el":"C"},{"x":2.108,"y":-0.674,"z":-0.064,"el":"C"},{"x":1.294,"y":1.601,"z":-0.049,"el":"C"},{"x":2.322,"y":0.687,"z":-0.281,"el":"C"},{"x":-0.637,"y":3.429,"z":0.371,"el":"C"},{"x":2.77,"y":-2.958,"z":-0.028,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.927,"y":-1.561,"z":-0.852,"el":"H"},{"